#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676e15e9-612f-5ed1-a846-28fe6d3e3ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245225} {:answer/id #uuid "a57396df-57ea-5c7f-b642-f9fd12f6ed01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245799} {:answer/id #uuid "af1b6f76-a3ca-5ecd-9112-eb60fc25b766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245876} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b5ef6b-a85e-5ed6-8d41-bc4cd9b40684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245926} {:answer/id #uuid "f7593b64-2bd8-57b1-b61f-c895725ba3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "676e15e9-612f-5ed1-a846-28fe6d3e3ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245225} :answer-field/id #uuid "27eb5715-3f31-5abf-98b3-d7de4753d25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3abf2741-714d-5beb-ba16-e544e0368941" :question/math-academy-id "q-110341" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+5x+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+5x+2 & =2 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+5x+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521bb03d-1d14-507e-b5f1-abf1722f95ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521bb03d-1d14-507e-b5f1-abf1722f95ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295739} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3516820-e93c-50fa-892a-8f84d07c3eb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f15cb00-451b-5523-bf6f-8460aecf67b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-312584" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41e761f-f00d-5ff2-b6d3-4945c96a48d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d41e761f-f00d-5ff2-b6d3-4945c96a48d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316308} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc60e108-fe27-56bc-ab5b-e81061889adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01236ef1-a695-5a6b-b74a-3c72e53e2a80" :question/math-academy-id "q-342582" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1654af3a-6a54-5bad-a90a-1d04a1eac607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342435} {:answer/id #uuid "177a28fc-5d5d-5178-bb19-3db2fba810ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342446} {:answer/id #uuid "3b40d316-481b-585e-b034-5019889d7f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342723} {:answer/id #uuid "556dc4a5-3d53-5af6-80f9-fe4cc1996296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342947} {:answer/id #uuid "e8efca49-b1d0-5e35-8e0a-b10689abd20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b40d316-481b-585e-b034-5019889d7f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342723} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda22a7c-59b1-5098-a010-5775c2ec3e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3888632a-e872-565e-a652-d98abc5852ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-75832" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(7).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $9$ | $-1$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ | $-4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(7)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(7)=4$ and ${g}^{′}(4)=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb7d97b-8ad8-525a-a7ed-77ea58145c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343005} {:answer/id #uuid "716f0289-aeaa-5e67-84c1-81f3b2fb048a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343215} {:answer/id #uuid "85645fd8-88a6-5000-b519-8bb725cb257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343380} {:answer/id #uuid "8785fc80-bda9-5f80-a872-d827ec5de9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343411} {:answer/id #uuid "bc91e678-6e8a-5bd2-8014-4d6dc79dba2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85645fd8-88a6-5000-b519-8bb725cb257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343380} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaa2dacc-4ebe-5f8c-9015-7703ef1d6e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf2118c1-1a12-50d3-8679-f4bc4826e598" :question/math-academy-id "q-76112" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=-2$ since the logarithm is not defined for negative numbers. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{2(x+2)}{2x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)+\\frac{2(0+2)}{2\\cdot 0+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =0+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
